<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # It's Our Garden – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Our Garden.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Pink Flowers!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Match simple words to corresponding pictures. Reading: Identify simple words for very common symbols. Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can match nature-related words with primary or secondary colors to corresponding pictures (e.g., "pink flowers," "green tree," "yellow leaves"). Can recognize written words for nature symbols when supported by pictures (e.g., leaves, seeds). Can recognize sounds of letters related to nature-related vocabulary when supported with visuals (e.g., identifying the /s/ sound in "sun" when shown a picture of the sun or the /t/ sound in "tree" when shown an image of a tree).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2; border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards with pictures of garden items previously introduced (e.g., a "pink flower", "green tree", "sun", "leaf", "seed"). Teacher says the word for each picture. Students repeat in chorus. Then, the teacher shows word cards (e.g., "flower", "tree", "sun") and students point to the corresponding picture flashcard. The teacher then asks, "What color is the flower?" to elicit "pink." (Pragmatic: Asking questions in nature).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with a picture of a "sun" and a word card "sun". Teacher points to the word and clearly says "/s/ /u/ /n/, sun". Then, the teacher holds up a picture of a "tree" and a word card "tree", demonstrating "/t/ /r/ /ee/, tree". The teacher repeats this for "garden" (/g/ sound). The teacher then shows a simple sentence like "The flowers are pink." (Grammar: Noun + verb + adjective) and points to each word as they read it aloud, emphasizing matching the written word to its meaning.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing picture of a flower and the word "flower") "Is this a tree or a flower?" (Expected: "A flower!")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing the word "pink") "What color is 'pink'?" (Students point to something pink or show a pink color swatch).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, illustrated 'big book' or projected slides showing simple sentences about a garden, such as:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">This is our garden.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The flowers are pink.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The tree is tall.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">See the green leaf.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The sun is big.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I like plants! (Sociolinguistic: Respect for nature)</li>
                </ul>
            Genre: Simple narrative text with high-frequency vocabulary. Register: Informal and descriptive. Format: Picture book pages/slides.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students can understand very short, simple sentences about familiar topics when supported by pictures.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> After reading the whole story, "What is this story about?" (Expected: "A garden!" or "Flowers!").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (using pictures):</b> The teacher reads a sentence, e.g., "The flowers are green." and shows a picture of pink flowers. Students show thumbs up for True or thumbs down for False. (Reading: Match simple words to pictures).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Teacher shows two sentences on the board (e.g., "The sun is big." and "The tree is tall.") and points to two corresponding pictures. Students draw a line from the sentence to the correct picture.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice matching written words (nouns: garden, tree, flower, leaf, sun; adjectives: pink, green, tall, big) to corresponding pictures and identifying initial letter sounds in words like "sun" (/s/) and "tree" (/t/). Grammar focus: Noun + verb + adjective (e.g., "The flowers are pink.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice - Picture-Word Match:</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of a garden, pink flowers, a green tree, the sun, and a leaf. Below the pictures are a scrambled list of target words (e.g., "flower", "tree", "sun", "pink", "green"). Students draw a line from each word to its matching picture. (Reading: Match simple words to corresponding pictures).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> Students receive another worksheet with simple sentences and a missing word, along with a word bank and a guiding picture. Example: "The _____ are pink." (picture of pink flowers). Word bank: [tree, flowers, sun]. Students choose and write the correct word. This activity also includes a phonemic awareness focus: "Circle the words that start with the /s/ sound." (e.g., sun, seed).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction - Read and Check:</b> Students work in pairs. They exchange their completed worksheets from activity 1. Partner A reads a word (e.g., "flower") and Partner B points to the picture and says the word. Then they switch roles. For activity 2, they read their completed sentences to each other, checking if the words match the pictures.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Garden Labels" - Students will create a simple labeled picture of a garden scene and read their labels.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive a large, outline drawing of a garden scene (Scenario: "It's Our Garden.") with various elements like a big sun, a tall tree, pink flowers, green leaves, and an area for soil. They also receive a sheet of small word cards to cut out: "sun", "tree", "pink flowers", "green leaf", "soil".</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">First, students color their garden picture, making sure the flowers are pink and the tree/leaves are green (Theme: "The Pink Flowers!"). Then, they cut out the word cards and paste each word next to the corresponding item in their garden picture. (Reading: Match simple words to pictures; identify simple words for common symbols like "soil").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Once completed, students work in pairs. Each student shows their labeled garden picture to their partner and points to an item, reading its label aloud (e.g., pointing to the flowers and saying, "Pink flowers."). Their partner listens and confirms. The teacher circulates, listening to students' reading and providing support. (Grammar: Noun + adjective phrases).</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "My Garden Labels" activity and peer assessment during reading in pairs. The teacher uses a simple checklist to note if students can correctly match words to pictures and read them aloud. </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete their "My Garden Labels" picture. The teacher collects these for review. Students also perform the pair reading activity, demonstrating their ability to read the labels. (Reading: Can match nature-related words with primary or secondary colors to corresponding pictures).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly matches words to pictures in the garden scene.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reads labels intelligibly to a partner.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows recognition of words for nature symbols (e.g., leaf, seed).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., "pink flowers," "green tree") correctly.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw a quick picture of their favorite garden item and write the word for it. Then they answer:</p>
            <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. I can match words to pictures: Yes / A little / No</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. I can read "pink flowers": Yes / A little / No</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new word did you like to read today?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy or hard about matching words to pictures?</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The picture-word matching activities worked very well for most students, showing good progress in reading simple words. The phonemic awareness integration (identifying /s/ and /t/ sounds) was effective with visual support. Some students needed more individual support in decoding unfamiliar words independently, requiring more one-on-one modeling. For the next lesson, I will provide more opportunities for students to orally describe their garden pictures using the target vocabulary before moving to speaking activities, possibly introducing simple sentence frames like "This is my ___." and "My ___ is ___." to build confidence for Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading and recognizing garden vocabulary. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on this by having students verbally describe their gardens and ask questions about others' garden pictures using the vocabulary and grammar learned (e.g., "What color is your flower?").</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>